---
import LogoHorizontal from "./svg/LogoHorizontal.astro";
import LogoVertical from "./svg/LogoVertical.astro";
import LogoIcon from "./svg/LogoIcon.astro";
---

<div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl space-y-16 px-4 py-12">
  <section class="space-y-4">
    <p class="text-eyebrow text-accent">Brand &amp; Typography System</p>
    <h1 class="text-h1">
      Writing that carries the Confederation
      <LogoIcon class="inline-block h-[0.9em] w-auto align-[-0.12em]" /> mark
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lead">
      This page pairs our predefined type styles with the Confederation College
      logos so you can see how real content behaves. Every heading, paragraph,
      and accent below uses the same utility classes you&rsquo;ll reach for when
      building pages &mdash; nothing here is bespoke.
    </p>
    <p class="text-p">
      The mark above is the <span class="font-mono text-sm">&lt;LogoIcon /&gt;</span
      > component set at the current text size. Because it inherits
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">currentColor</span>, it flows inside a
      sentence like any other glyph, which makes it handy for inline callouts and
      lead-ins without breaking the reading rhythm.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <h2 class="text-h2">Section headings and the horizontal lockup (H2)</h2>
    <p class="text-p">
      Use <span class="font-mono text-sm">text-h2</span> for major section
      breaks. When a section needs to stand for the institution as a whole, the
      horizontal lockup sits naturally at the top of it &mdash; the wordmark&rsquo;s
      weight balances against the heading below.
    </p>
    <LogoHorizontal class="h-12 w-auto" />
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <h3 class="text-h3">Pairing type with the vertical lockup (H3)</h3>
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-6 sm:flex-row sm:items-start">
      <LogoVertical class="h-28 w-auto shrink-0" />
      <p class="text-p">
        <span class="font-mono text-sm">text-h3</span> suits subsections and
        important points. The vertical lockup earns its place beside body copy
        &mdash; in a sidebar, a card, or a signature block &mdash; where its
        stacked proportions read well in a narrow column. Notice how the heading
        stays dominant while the mark supports rather than competes with it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <h4 class="text-h4">Fourth level heading (H4)</h4>
    <p class="text-p">
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">text-h4</span> organizes more detailed
      information. At this level the heading still stands apart from body text
      while beginning to approach its size, so it works for the smaller
      structural blocks within a longer article.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <h5 class="text-h5">Fifth level heading (H5)</h5>
    <p class="text-p">
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">text-h5</span> is typically used for minor
      sections or callouts. The visual distinction becomes more subtle here,
      relying on weight as much as size &mdash; a good pairing for labelled
      groups of links or metadata.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <h6 class="text-h6">Sixth level heading (H6)</h6>
    <p class="text-p">
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">text-h6</span> is the smallest heading,
      useful for fine detail or specialized content. At this size the difference
      from paragraph text is often just font weight, so reserve it for genuinely
      low-level structure.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <figure class="space-y-4">
      <LogoIcon class="h-10 w-auto text-accent" />
      <blockquote class="text-quote italic">
        Good typography is invisible &mdash; it lets the reader forget the type
        and remember the idea. Our styles and marks exist to serve the message,
        never to upstage it.
      </blockquote>
    </figure>
    <p class="text-small">
      The <span class="font-mono text-xs">text-quote</span> style stands out from
      regular paragraphs while staying in harmony with the system. The mark is a
      single-color logo &mdash; here it inherits the brand navy through
      <span class="font-mono text-xs">currentColor</span>, so it always matches
      the type it sits beside.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <p class="text-p">
      This is a standard paragraph (<span class="font-mono text-sm">text-p</span
      >) demonstrating the base text style. It should be highly readable and
      comfortable for extended reading &mdash; the workhorse for nearly all
      content on a page.
    </p>
    <p class="text-small">
      This is small text (<span class="font-mono text-xs">text-small</span>) for
      captions, footnotes, or supplementary information such as image credits and
      legal lines.
    </p>
    <button class="text-button rounded bg-accent px-4 py-2 text-white"
      >Button Text Style</button
    >
  </section>
</div>
